From 210c1d7aa5812402053243cdf08ffc3e473ec820 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:40:38 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] dsv41: TF_DS_DEVICE_ACCEPT (off by default) computes a
 chain window's keep on the device - the foundational piece of an on-device
 accept

Switch: TF_DS_DEVICE_ACCEPT ("1" or "on"; anything else or unset: off). Read by rank 0's lane at init on every rank alike; the switch carries no fingerprint bit and no switches-frame bit: it changes only which side compares, and an uneven pair is still bit-identical (the follower never reads the keep). Greedy and device-draw rounds only: a host_draw round (speculative sampling's p off the host logits) never calls the pick kernel's accept path, and a tree window (Window.parents != null) never gets the device keep; both keep the host rule, byte for byte as the base.

The accept rule (core/lanes/accept.zig's acceptPath) for a CHAIN window (no parents; row i's parent row i - 1) is the identity prefix 0..keep where keep is the longest prefix r >= 1 with tokens[r] == picks[r - 1], keep in [1, rows]. Today the picks come back (sync + D2H of R u32 into amax_host) and the host's conclude walks the window to rebuild that path. This patch computes the keep on the device, bit-identically: a new tf_ds_accept_chain_kernel (kernels/cuda/dsv41_ops.cu; one block per window, 32 threads, one thread walks R <= 16 rows) runs right after the pick kernel in roundArgmax, reading the same buffers the forward already wrote (Round.inputs' int64 window ids; the picks at amax), and lands one u32 keep per window right after the picks in amax (keeps at amax + R * 4). The same D2H then carries R + nwin u32s, and verifyFn stores each window's keep in its Verified's new device_keep field. conclude takes it: for a non-forced window whose keep is in range and whose parent array is a chain, the path is chainPath(keep) (the first keep rows) instead of acceptPath; anything else - an out-of-range keep, a tree, an absent one - takes acceptPath, unchanged. The keeps and the picks never cross ranks: each rank reads the same frames as before, and the bytes on the wire (and the reply) are the same as the base, set or unset.

Sizing. amax and amax_host grew by max_streams (16) u32 keep slots, not by this patch's design sketch's +4: the workspace bound for windows per round is max_streams everywhere (round.max_rows = 48 rows + 16 keeps * 4 B = 256 B, one small arena take). With the sketch's +4, a shared round of more than four windows (allowed: --parallel goes to 16) would have let the kernel write past the amax slot into whatever the bump allocator lays out next (arena.take has no guard). The memcpy stays (R + nwin) * 4; an unset switch launches nothing and memcpy's exactly the base's R * 4.

What this is not. It does not make the draft pass one frame. The depth rule (rule.headDepth) is rank 0's host state; the cuts (the thinking budget, the loop guard, the call gate) are Stream host state evaluated on the committed tokens inside conclude; the T26 keyed-draft keys exist only in the pass frame; trees need the parent array uploaded (chains need only row0/counts). Baseten's one-graph steps (spec_accept, crates/kernels/cu/ops.cu:591, capture_spec_blk, capture_spec_vadapt) read committed tokens from a snapshot two steps back, which maps onto Baseten's one-stream loop, not onto this engine's two-frame round with rank 1 a follower of every frame. This patch is the foundational piece - the accept's keep, on the device, for the windows that need no more state than row offsets - plus a measure of completeness: with it, device accept is exactly as complete as the depth rule and the cuts allow, and that is still short of a one-frame round.

LOSSY: no. Bits identical: the device computes the same integer the host would (the longest matching prefix), the path is the same, the committed tokens and the reply are the same. exact.py must stay IDENTICAL with the switch on; if it does not, the kernel is wrong and this does not ship. Gates on the pair, in order: exact.py (IDENTICAL expected), then tf-dsv41-lanes --profile and the served process's TF_DS_ROUND_PROFILE /metrics line (0123) for the send/logits/sample shares - if small, stop; this patch alone does not move them much (it removes the host acceptPath walk, not the sync). TF_DS_DEVICE_ACCEPT_CHECK was considered and dropped: equivalence is covered by host tests (accept.zig's chain property test over all 0/1 token windows up to 6 rows pins acceptPath to the identity prefix; engine.zig's chainPath test pins the identity by length; conclude falls back to acceptPath on any out-of-range keep or non-chain parent array) and by exact.py on the pair. A design note rides at docs/device-accept.md in the Sparks repo (untracked there).

Tests (host): accept.zig's a chain's acceptPath is the identity prefix over the matching drafts (exhaustive 0/1 tokens, 1..6 rows) and engine.zig's chainPath takes a window's first keep rows, alongside the existing suites; the device path itself needs the pair. The .cu gets only a syntax check on this host: clang++ -fsyntax-only on the extracted kernel under a plain-C++ stub (no CUDA toolkit on this host: no nvcc, no libdevice; the -x cuda frontend cannot run) passes; the image's nvcc build on the Sparks is the first real compile of the new kernel. Host gates: `zig build test` (one reported spurious "failed command" on this host, pre-existing flaky big binaries; that binary - 408/408 - and every .zig-cache/o test binary pass standalone), and `zig build install native -Dfatbins=/home/colum/.cache/tf-dummy-fatbins` succeeds (the CUDA path type-checks; the .cu itself is not compiled here). Not run here: any pair, any smoke, exact.py.

